= Decompress =

'''1.''' Change directory.

The decompression command below must be run from within the folder where you downloaded the archive (most likely in <code>~/Downloads</code> or <code>~/</code> (home) folder).

<u>Note:</u> Adjust the folder if you stored the archive elsewhere.

{{CodeSelect|code=
cd ~/Downloads
}}

'''2.''' Do not use <code>unxz</code>! Extract the images using GNU <code>tar</code>.

'''3.''' Make sure the <code>xz-utils</code> package is installed on your system. 
<ref>
https://forums.whonix.org/t/tar-child-xz-cannot-exec-no-such-file-or-directory-install-xz-utils-package/16708/7
</ref>

If you followed the installation instructions above, this should already be the case.

'''4.''' Decompress.

If your filesystem <u>does</u> support [[Sparse_files|Sparse Files]] <ref>
tar --extract --sparse --verbose --file=Kicksecure*.libvirt.xz
</ref>:

{{CodeSelect|code=
tar -xSvf Kicksecure*.libvirt.xz
}}

If your filesystem does <u>not</u> support sparse files:

{{CodeSelect|code=
tar -xvf Kicksecure*.libvirt.xz
}}

'''5.''' Wait.

Be aware that the extraction process may take an exceptionally long time to complete. It is recommended to allow the terminal to run uninterrupted after executing the command to ensure successful decompression.

'''6.''' Done.

= Importing {{project_name_short}} VM Template =

The supplied XML files serve as a description for libvirt and define the properties of a {{project_name_short}} VM and the networking it should have.

* Define the properties of {{project_name_short}} VM

{{CodeSelect|code=
virsh -c qemu:///session define {{project_name_short}}*.xml
}}

= Tell libvirt to stop trying to adjust resources limits and force libvirt to skip it =

Libvirt tries to set the VM process maximum core dump file size to "unlimited", but the system will blocks it because unprivileged users not allowed to raise their own hard resource limits (setrlimit).

Since there is no root access to modify system-wide limits, then we need to tell libvirt to stop trying to adjust this limit by forcing libvirt to skip resource limits.

So to override the memory/core limits for QEMU inside user configuration directory:

* Create new directory:

{{CodeSelect|code=
mkdir -p ~/.config/libvirt/
}}

* Make and edit new file:

{{CodeSelect|code=
nano ~/.config/libvirt/qemu.conf
}}

* Copy and Paste:

<pre>
max_core = 0
max_processes = 0
max_files = 0
</pre>

* Save and exit {{Keypress|Ctrl|x}} then {{Keypress|y}} then {{Keypress|Enter}}.

* Reboot your system so the changes can take effect.

* Done

= Image File Installation =
The XML files are configured to use newly created user level path <code>~/.local/share/images</code> instead of the default storage location of <code>/var/lib/libvirt/images</code>. The image files can be moved or copied, depending on storage constraints.

<u>Notes:</u>

* The directory must be created before copying or moving the images. To create the path <ref>Another less practical and not guaranteed method could be used by running <code>virt-manager -c qemu:///session</code>. This will open virt-manager, triggering the default storage path, and the user will see the {{project_name_short}} image there. However, if the user tries to run it, it will not work because the image has not yet been transferred to the storage path (so it has a practical issue if the user is not aware of this). Also, there is no guarantee that it will work in every case on every distribution.</ref>:

{{CodeSelect|code=
mkdir -p ~/.local/share/images
}}

* The following steps move or copy the images so the virtual machines can boot.

Either move <u>or</u> copy.
{{Tab
|type=controller
|content=
{{Tab
|title= === Moving {{project_name_short}} Image Files ===
|type=section
|active=true
|content=
Move the image files.

{{CodeSelect|code=
mv {{project_name_short}}*.qcow2 ~/.local/share/images/{{project_name_short}}.qcow2
}}
}}
{{Tab
|title= === Copying {{project_name_short}} Image Files ===
|content=
Copy the image files. {{Anchor|sparse files}}

{{project_name_short}} disk images are [[Sparse_files|sparse files]], meaning they expand as data is written rather than allocating their entire size (100GB) outright. Sparse files require special commands when they are copied to ensure they retain this property; otherwise, they will occupy the full disk space.

{{CodeSelect|code=
cp --sparse=always {{project_name_short}}*.qcow2 ~/.local/share/images/{{project_name_short}}.qcow2
}}<!-- close CodeSelect -->
}}<!-- close tab -->
}}<!-- close controller -->

== Start ==

=== Virtual Machine Manager (Virt-Manager) GUI default is <code>qemu:///system</code> not <code>qemu:///session</code> ===

If {{project_name_short}} VM not shown in the default opening of Virt-Manager, Then this is because it assumes your VMs on default <code>qemu:///system</code>, so you need to change that to <code>qemu:///session</code>:

Go to <code>Virtual Machine Manager</code> &rarr; <code>File</code> &rarr; <code>Add Connection...</code> &rarr; Switch Hypervisor from <code>QEMU/KVM</code> to <code>QEMU/KVM user session</code> &rarr; Tick <code>Autoconnect</code> &rarr; <code>Connect</code>.

Images:

[[File:enable-qemu-session-1.png|500px]]

[[File:enable-qemu-session-2.png|500px]]

[[File:enable-qemu-session-3.png|500px]]

[[File:enable-qemu-session-4.png|500px]]

After that start {{project_name_long}} VM.

----

If the virt-manager always going to show <code>qemu:///system</code> whenever you start the virt-manager, then to solve this run:

{{CodeSelect|code=
echo "export LIBVIRT_DEFAULT_URI='qemu:///session'" >> ~/.zshrc
}}
{{CodeSelect|code=
source ~/.zshrc
}}

(If someone is using Bash instead of Zsh, change <code>.zshrc</code> to <code>.bashrc</code>, This tells your entire user profile including the GUI to always target your local user session by default).

=== Using Kicksecure with LXQt as Graphical User Interface (GUI) Desktop ===

Start Virtual Machine Manager:

<code>Start Menu</code> &rarr; <code>System Tools</code> &rarr; <code>Virtual Machine Manager</code>

Start {{project_name_short}}:

<code>click on {{project_name_short}}</code> &rarr; <code>click Open</code> &rarr; <code>click the play symbol</code>

= Inserting dGPU (e.g Nvidia) directly to VM =

One of the unique features for KVM is that it allows the insertion of the host dedicated/discrete GPU (e.g Nvidia) into the VM directly instead of only virtualized graphics.

There are hardware and software requirements needs to be available: 

== Hardware ==

'''1.''' Make sure you have [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integrated_GPU#Integrated_graphics_processing_unit Integrated GPU] (built-in GPU in your processor). Most PCs are coming with iGPU if it has dGPU (hybrid) by default but sometimes thats not the case <ref>https://forums.kicksecure.com/t/black-login-screen-crash-due-to-nouveau-gsp-killing-labwc/1556/17</ref>. In order to check that run:

{{CodeSelect|code=
lspci -Dnnk {{!}} grep -A4 -E 'VGA{{!}}3D{{!}}Audio'
}}

Expected output as an example:

<pre>
VGA compatible controller [0300]: Intel Raptor Lake-S UHD Graphics
</pre>

'''2.''' Make sure you have dGPU available, you can run the same command and it will show as e.g:

<pre>
0000:01:00.0 VGA compatible controller [0300]: NVIDIA Corporation AD107M [Geforce RTX 4060 Max-Q / Mobile] [10de:28a0]
        kernel driver in use: nouveau
0000:01:00.1 Audio device [0403]: NVIDIA Corporation AD107 High Definition Audio Controller [10de:22be] 
</pre>

Note: These IDs/addresses <code>0000:01:00.0</code>, <code>0000:01:00.1</code>, <code>10de:28a0</code>, <code>10de:22be</code> we are going to use them later.

'''3.''' Check the support of Input-output memory management ([https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Input%E2%80%93output_memory_management_unit IOMMU]) support:

{{CodeSelect|code=
grep --color=auto -E iommu /proc/cmdline
}}

The important output in case you have Intel:

<pre>
intel_iommu=on
</pre>

== Software ==

=== SYSMAINT side (root/admin) ===

'''1.''' Switch to [[Nvidia#Software_Rendering_(Pixman)|software rendering (Pixman)]].

'''2.''' [[Nvidia#Debian_Repository|Install Nvidia]] and make sure its [[Nvidia#Check_NVIDIA_Functionality|recognized]] by SYSMAINT (optional to make sure your card is working properly before inserting it or you want to switch back later).

Then follow these steps:

==== GRUB ====

* Add these parameters to GRUB 

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudoedit /etc/default/grub.d/50_user.cfg
}}

* Then add:

{{CodeSelect|code=
GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="$GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX nouveau.noaccel=1 intel_iommu=on vfio-pci.ids=10de:28a0,10de:22be"
}}

==== Blacklist the host from loading NVIDIA drivers ====

* Creat and edit this file in modprobe.d: 

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudoedit /etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-nvidia.conf
}}

* Add these entries to blacklist: 

{{CodeSelect|code=
blacklist nouveau
blacklist nvidia
blacklist nvidia_drm
blacklist nvidia_modeset
blacklist nvidia_uvm
}}

==== Configure dracut to load VFIO early ====

* Creat and edit this file in modprobe.d: 

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudoedit /etc/dracut.conf.d/vfio.conf
}}

* Add (Keep the spaces inside the quotation marks):

{{CodeSelect|code=
force_drivers+=" vfio vfio_pci vfio_iommu_type1 "
omit_drivers+=" nouveau nvidia nvidia_drm nvidia_modeset nvidia_uvm "
}}

<code>force_drivers</code> makes dracut attempt early module loading, <code>omit_drivers</code> excludes the listed drivers from the generated initramfs.

==== Give the account KVM access and create the VFIO access group ====

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudo groupadd -f vfio
}}

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudo usermod -aG vfio,kvm user
}}

==== Configure memory-lock limits ====

* per-account PAM login limits

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudoedit /etc/security/limits.d/99-memlock.conf
}}

* Add:

{{CodeSelect|code=
user soft memlock unlimited
user hard memlock unlimited
}}

* libvirt launched through the user's systemd manager

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudo systemctl edit "user@$(id -u user).service"
}}

* Add at the bottom:

{{CodeSelect|code=
[Service]
LimitMEMLOCK=infinity
}}

==== Rebuild the initramfs and GRUB, then reboot ====

===== GRUB =====

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudo update-grub
}}

===== Dracut =====

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudo dracut --force --regenerate-all 
}}

===== Reboot =====

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudo reboot
}}

* Then boot into SYSMAINT again.

==== Create persistent permissions for the verified group ====

{{CodeSelect|code=
ls -l /dev/vfio
}}

* The output should be something similar to:

<pre>
crw-rw---- 1 root vfio 245,   0  Sep 21 12:58 17
crw-rw-rw- 1 root root  10, 196  Sep 21 12:58 vfio
</pre>

* The important number is we are looking for in our example here is <code>17</code>.

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudoedit /etc/udev/rules.d/70-vfio-user.rules
}}

* Add:

{{CodeSelect|code=
SUBSYSTEM=="vfio", KERNEL=="17", OWNER="root", GROUP="vfio", MODE="0660"
}}

* Then reboot:

{{CodeSelect|code=
sudo reboot
}}

=== User ===

* Boot into user, and go to virt-manager main window (the window listing your virtual machines) then open:

<code> Edit -> Preferences -> General -> tick "Enable XML editing" </code>

* After that press on the VM that you want to attach the GPU to, then press on Open button, then press on the bulb (Show virtual hardware details), Go to the bottom and press on Add Hardware, Go to PCI Host Devices then click on your graphic card then press on Finish. 

* Go to the added PCI/graphic card then press on XML change <code>managed="yes"</code> to <code>managed="no"</code>, then press on Apply.

* After that run your VM and the graphic card should be appeared in the VM (dont forget to install the graphic card driver in the VM as well).

* Done

= Uninstall =

<div class="toccolours mw-collapsible mw-collapsed">
If you want to remove {{project_name_short}} KVM VM, the {{project_name_short}} network, and {{project_name_short}} image, click Expand on the right.

<div class="mw-collapsible-content">

{{Box|text=
'''1.''' Power off the VM you want to Remove (If not already).

{{CodeSelect|code=
virsh -c qemu:///session destroy {{project_name_short}}
}}

'''2.''' Remove the KVM VM settings.

{{CodeSelect|code=
virsh -c qemu:///session undefine {{project_name_short}}
}}

'''3.''' Delete the images.

<u>Note:</u> All data will be lost unless it is backed up first. Adjust the paths to the images as appropriate.

{{CodeSelect|code=
safe-rm ~/.local/share/images/{{project_name_short}}.qcow2
}}
}}
</div>
</div>

